Yes. This is the right checkpoint now: **stop treating Oracle and WorkerKit as two products and make one worker repeatedly use the Oracle, remember what it has already learned about the opportunity landscape, choose work, execute it, and feed the outcome back into its own decision model.**

The latest `/mw` is already capable of being the evidence spine. WorkerKit has WorkOrders, append-only events, costs, verification, submission/outcome/settlement receipts, capability evidence and versioned processes; your own handover explicitly identifies the next milestone as one real Oracle opportunity completing that lifecycle.

The Oracle is also further along conceptually than its runtime implementation. Its spec already has the correct raw-first structure—sources → immutable observations → canonical opportunities → observations/events → skills/categories → metrics—and explicitly treats Oracle opportunities as future labor demand and WorkerKit workers/capabilities as future supply.

The main work now is **joining them without corrupting the clean layers**.

