# 9. And yes: scratch memory is absolutely a real distinct primitive

The recent memory literature strongly supports separating **temporary working state** from long-term memory.

A 2026 survey of long-horizon agents explicitly distinguishes long context, long-horizon execution and cross-task long-term memory—they are different system properties. ([arXiv][1])

Older cognitive architectures make a similar functional split: working memory is temporary task-related state, while long-term memory includes semantic facts, procedural rules and episodic experiences. ([arXiv][2])

For Moltwork I would use **five memory classes**.

```text
SCRATCH
"What am I currently thinking about?"

EPISODIC
"What happened before?"

SEMANTIC
"What do I believe to be true?"

PROCEDURAL
"How do I do this?"

GRAPH / RELATIONAL
"How do these things relate?"
```

And then Git separately handles:

```text
VERSION HISTORY
"How has my productive machinery changed?"
```

